WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.945
<v Instructor>So let's keep on learning about CloudFront.</v>

2
00:00:02.945 --> 00:00:04.688
So first we have geo restriction

3
00:00:04.688 --> 00:00:07.530
to restrict who can access your CloudFront distribution.

4
00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:08.730
So we have an allow list

5
00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:12.321
to say only users from these countries

6
00:00:12.321 --> 00:00:13.737
can access CloudFront,

7
00:00:13.737 --> 00:00:15.330
or we can have a block list

8
00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.470
to block users from banned countries

9
00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:21.420
or countries that you don't want to access your origin.

10
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.050
So the country is determined

11
00:00:23.911 --> 00:00:26.521
by using a third-party Geo-IP database

12
00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:27.608
from within CloudFront.

13
00:00:27.608 --> 00:00:29.273
So the use case for geo restriction

14
00:00:29.273 --> 00:00:30.749
is to use copyright laws

15
00:00:30.749 --> 00:00:32.130
to control access to content.

16
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.513
And on top of restriction,

17
00:00:34.428 --> 00:00:37.900
you can just customize your content as well per geolocation,

18
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.290
because CloudFront automatically is going to add a header

19
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.173
to the request called CloudFront-Viewer-Country,

20
00:00:44.273 --> 00:00:46.031
and that is going to be accessible

21
00:00:46.031 --> 00:00:47.460
to your origin or to a function

22
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.413
such as Lambda@Edge.

23
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.270
In terms of pricing,

24
00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.730
well the Edge Locations are all around the world,

25
00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.050
and the cost of the data in each location can vary.

26
00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.540
So we have a price table

27
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.220
and it turns out that, well,

28
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.800
it goes from lower to higher, based on location.

29
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.180
So the cheapest countries are going

30
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.340
to be the US, Mexico, and Canada,

31
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.220
and the most expensive one is going to be India.

32
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.970
Therefore, we can define price classes in CloudFront

33
00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.810
to reduce the number of Edge Locations for cost reduction.

34
00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.190
So you have three price classes,

35
00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.660
the first one, is called All.

36
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:22.740
When you have all the regions

37
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:23.970
that gives you the best performance,

38
00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.970
but obviously it's the highest cost.

39
00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.710
Then we have the Class 200.

40
00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.210
It's most regions,

41
00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:33.180
but it will exclude the most expensive regions.

42
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.190
And then we have Class 100,

43
00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:38.490
to only have the least expensive regions.

44
00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:41.400
So this is the table of inclusions

45
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.010
of each price class and each location.

46
00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.510
So you can have a look in your own time.

47
00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:47.820
But from a global perspective,

48
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.560
this is Price Class 100,

49
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.210
so it's the cheapest one.

50
00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.430
Then we have Price Class 200,

51
00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.220
and finally, we have the Price Class All,

52
00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.383
with all the Edge Locations.

53
00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.890
Now, CloudFront has signed URL,

54
00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.990
so the idea is that you wanna give

55
00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.120
a signed URL with an expiration

56
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.970
to control access to content in CloudFront.

57
00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.230
So the idea is that, for example,

58
00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:13.200
we have CloudFront that is integrated with Amazon S3

59
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.670
through an OAC.

60
00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:15.870
And an application server

61
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.870
that wants to access some content on CloudFront,

62
00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.610
but it needs to be protected.

63
00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:21.443
So what's going to happen

64
00:02:21.443 --> 00:02:23.460
is that the clients will need

65
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:27.750
to talk to an application server to generate a signed URL

66
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:28.740
with a trusted signer.

67
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.020
So the clients will talk to your application,

68
00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.750
will do authentication and authorization,

69
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:34.980
and if it's all good,

70
00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.330
then your application server

71
00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.550
is going to generate a signed URL

72
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.550
using the SDK onto CloudFront,

73
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.580
and return that signed URL to the client.

74
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.370
Then the client can directly access CloudFront

75
00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.110
by using the signed URL

76
00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.690
and getting the specific bit of content

77
00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.460
from your Amazon S3 bucket.

78
00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.280
So you would do this so that your Amazon CloudFront

79
00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.350
origin distribution, sorry,

80
00:02:58.350 --> 00:02:59.700
is not publicly accessible,

81
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.250
doesn't have everything accessible to everyone,

82
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.850
but instead, some files must be accessed or a signed URL

83
00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.010
for enhanced security.

84
00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:09.240
So you may ask me, what's the difference

85
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.620
between using a signed URL

86
00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.320
and an S3 pre-signed URL?

87
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.780
Well, CloudFront signed URL allows you

88
00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.000
to access a path no matter what the origin is,

89
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.400
so it doesn't apply only to S3.

90
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.840
And then to sign these URL,

91
00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.400
there is an account wide key-pair

92
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.950
that only the root accounts can manage.

93
00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.410
So you can filter by IP, by path, by date,

94
00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:29.310
you can add an expiration,

95
00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.070
so they can be quite complicated for the signed URL.

96
00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:33.630
And you can leverage caching features

97
00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.570
so that the signed URL may just access content

98
00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:38.820
cashed on Edge Location.

99
00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.120
The S3 pre-signed URL is just to issue a request

100
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.460
as the person who pre-signed the URL.

101
00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:45.293
So in that case,

102
00:03:45.293 --> 00:03:49.140
you use the IAM key of the signing IAM principle.

103
00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.700
It has limited lifetime,

104
00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:51.533
and then you can use it

105
00:03:51.533 --> 00:03:53.160
to upload and download as well.

106
00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.983
So this is what a pre-signed URL looks like.

107
00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.410
Okay, next on CloudFront,

108
00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.420
we can have custom error pages.

109
00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:01.253
So the idea is that you want

110
00:04:01.253 --> 00:04:03.000
to return an object to the viewer

111
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.770
when the origin returns an error code

112
00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:09.150
for example, a 400 or a 500 status code to CloudFront.

113
00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.430
For this, we're going to create custom error pages,

114
00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:15.150
and we can also cache them using a caching minimum TTL.

115
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.110
So let's have an example,

116
00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.600
we have a request made to CloudFront,

117
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:21.270
CloudFront forwards the request to your origin,

118
00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:26.270
with response to a 400 or 500 type of error message.

119
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:27.660
Then what's going to happen

120
00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.350
is that instead of sending that response to the client,

121
00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:33.180
we can have custom error pages

122
00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.780
and they can be stored, for example, on an S3 bucket.

123
00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.570
So that for example, if it was a 403,

124
00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.610
then the 403 would be shared

125
00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.770
as an HTML file to the Edge Location,

126
00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.140
which would put it in the cache, okay?

127
00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.900
And then sent back to the client.

128
00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.660
So we would have a customization of error pages

129
00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.460
because they don't come from the origin,

130
00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.940
they come from your S3 bucket instead.

131
00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:57.773
Okay.

132
00:04:57.773 --> 00:04:58.980
So that's it for CloudFront,

133
00:04:58.980 --> 00:04:59.813
I hope you liked it.

134
00:04:59.813 --> 00:05:01.740
And I will see you in the next lecture.

